# 128-bit floating point (IEEE 754 quad, ~34 decimal digits). No backend in this set has a portable native quad: JS and
# swift have only f64, kotlin only Double, and rust's f128 is unstable. So this module routes "quad precision" to the
# exact-base-10 `decimal` (unbounded mantissa), which is more than 34 digits of precision and portable everywhere. Use a
# native f128 directly on a backend that gains one; for exact decimal precision today, this is the answer.

load @cluesurf/base/code/decimal
  find big-decimal
  find make-big-decimal

# a quad-precision (here: exact arbitrary-precision base-10) float from its text
task make-quad
  take value, like text
  like big-decimal
  send back
    call make-big-decimal
      read value
